trying to get, do with your agents.

230
00:12:31,148 --> 00:12:33,818
So we built-- I'll tell you
what didn't work out for us.

231
00:12:34,538 --> 00:12:36,398
We built a scorecard for agent quality.

232
00:12:36,428 --> 00:12:38,928
It looked so professional,
but it was wrong.

233
00:12:39,418 --> 00:12:40,668
The formulas were fine.

234
00:12:40,678 --> 00:12:42,428
We fed them through the wrong data.

235
00:12:42,438 --> 00:12:45,548
Most of the spans we scored
were the tiny helper calls

236
00:12:45,568 --> 00:12:47,158
with almost no session context.

237
00:12:47,828 --> 00:12:51,768
We almost used that healthy score
to argue that the fleet was ready.

238
00:12:52,408 --> 00:12:55,358
The chart was not the bug, the
population behind the chart was.

239
00:12:56,468 --> 00:12:58,778
Missing data is not a passing grade.

240
00:12:59,268 --> 00:13:02,208
A blanket fie- a blank
field is not a green light.

241
00:13:02,398 --> 00:13:07,048
If there is no session ID, you cannot tell
five healthy job apart from one stuck job.

242
00:13:07,938 --> 00:13:11,888
If there were no token counts,
you cannot talk about cost.

243
00:13:12,038 --> 00:13:16,758
If there are no evidence checks, you do
not know whether the answer was grounded.

244
00:13:17,158 --> 00:13:20,598
If there was no stage label, you do
not know whether the plan gather or

245
00:13:21,118 --> 00:13:23,098
present failed, which section failed.

246
00:13:24,218 --> 00:13:26,118
So when the data is
incomplete, say unknown.

247
00:13:26,678 --> 00:13:32,152
Do not invent a high score, period
Rule measure telemetry quality

248
00:13:32,172 --> 00:13:33,922
before you measure agent quality.

249
00:13:34,232 --> 00:13:36,162
First, let's get the basics right.

250
00:13:36,822 --> 00:13:40,612
Judge the quality of your telemetry before
you judge the quality of the agents.

251
00:13:41,012 --> 00:13:44,592
Label the plan, gather, present
when the star-- span starts.

252
00:13:44,622 --> 00:13:45,932
Track cost per tool.

253
00:13:45,942 --> 00:13:51,212
One, only call a run correct when
the agent is backed by evidence.

254
00:13:51,732 --> 00:13:55,542
So when we ship our products,
people usually say, "Hey, this

255
00:13:55,572 --> 00:13:56,822
AI investigation went wrong."

256
00:13:57,002 --> 00:14:01,022
They remember the conversation, but they
do not remember the twelve execution IDs.

257
00:14:01,052 --> 00:14:05,712
Give them-- What we did was then give
them a downloadable artifact, which

258
00:14:05,722 --> 00:14:07,262
actually covers the whole thread.

259
00:14:08,252 --> 00:14:09,922
Now, so trace again.

260
00:14:10,172 --> 00:14:12,112
Start with the same trace you saw earlier.

261
00:14:12,352 --> 00:14:15,592
Look for three things first:
where it looped on a tool, how

262
00:14:15,592 --> 00:14:19,372
much it costs, whether the final
answer cited the tool results.

263
00:14:20,092 --> 00:14:22,082
We have something called debug zip.

264
00:14:22,132 --> 00:14:25,632
Inside of the debug zip, you'll see
like the manifest with the version

265
00:14:25,632 --> 00:14:30,352
of the agent, the graph the UI uses,
the event streams, so that what was

266
00:14:30,362 --> 00:14:33,852
streamed to the end user, so that you
can actually replay the whole thing.

267
00:14:34,712 --> 00:14:39,012
And it's the same information you would
see on a live watch page, but offline.

268
00:14:39,052 --> 00:14:43,022
You do not have to guess which
of the dozen runs was a bad one.

269
00:14:43,422 --> 00:14:47,092
So you'll see the execution JSON, which
contains all the DAG information from

270
00:14:47,102 --> 00:14:51,692
this tool call to this LLM call to this
MCP call to like-- it goes on like that.

271
00:14:52,162 --> 00:14:57,702
So it's one zip that contains
all the information in one go.

272
00:14:57,702 --> 00:15:00,212
So it can-- makes our
troubleshooting much easier.

273
00:15:01,252 --> 00:15:05,332
Now we can do… Now that we have a zip
for a particular session, we can actually

274
00:15:05,372 --> 00:15:06,992
do batch grading and product gates.

275
00:15:07,012 --> 00:15:11,022
Now we actually can look at all
those zip for a particular agent.

276
00:15:11,582 --> 00:15:12,782
We can actually download it.

277
00:15:12,822 --> 00:15:16,822
We can actually see, okay, if a s-- if
the information came through a Slack

278
00:15:16,822 --> 00:15:20,342
or a web UI, and if they both gave
different answers, then we can actually

279
00:15:20,352 --> 00:15:23,662
be-- it's easier for us to correlate,
oh, this-- in this particular thing,

280
00:15:23,672 --> 00:15:27,262
the context was missing, and this
particular, the context was the…

281
00:15:27,512 --> 00:15:32,102
So we can actually fix how the
the run sessions were different.

282
00:15:32,962 --> 00:15:36,772
So measure what you run measure what
your own product blocked, users from

283
00:15:37,092 --> 00:15:38,962
doing, then ship a gate in the product.

284
00:15:39,692 --> 00:15:42,872
So do not keep tweaking the
prompt and fingers crossed, no,

285
00:15:42,992 --> 00:15:43,982
this is gonna work out for you.

286
00:15:44,042 --> 00:15:46,952
So it's more, more than luck-driven
development, we can actually

287
00:15:46,952 --> 00:15:48,472
do evidence-based development.

288
00:15:50,458 --> 00:15:51,878
So starter set.

289
00:15:52,428 --> 00:15:53,928
Why did the agent do that?

290
00:15:54,228 --> 00:15:57,698
This is just if anything from
this talk, please take this

291
00:15:57,708 --> 00:15:59,168
home, these are the six signals.

292
00:15:59,288 --> 00:16:02,228
None of them are, is the pod up, okay?

293
00:16:02,328 --> 00:16:05,478
If you only do two things
next week, do this.

294
00:16:05,498 --> 00:16:10,218
First, alert when one session costs
much more than the agent's recent

295
00:16:10,218 --> 00:16:14,198
averages, and when the tool cost
count is far above normal run.

296
00:16:14,688 --> 00:16:18,778
Second, do not mark a run as
good unless the final answer

297
00:16:18,778 --> 00:16:20,718
cites what gathers returned.

298
00:16:21,098 --> 00:16:25,928
That is the cheapest way to go from green
dashboards to why did the agent do that?

299
00:16:25,958 --> 00:16:29,868
There is longer checklist on
the blog productionnotes.dev.

300
00:16:30,218 --> 00:16:33,128
This table is enough to open a
ticket for Monday to actually get

301
00:16:33,128 --> 00:16:34,708
some confidence in the agents.

302
00:16:36,108 --> 00:16:36,838
Five lessons.

303
00:16:37,008 --> 00:16:39,498
I'll re- leave the full
list on the screen.

304
00:16:39,518 --> 00:16:41,208
Here is the short version on out loud.

305
00:16:42,198 --> 00:16:43,948
Watch spend early.

306
00:16:43,978 --> 00:16:46,028
Trace plan gather and present.

307
00:16:46,358 --> 00:16:47,878
Track cost per tool.

308
00:16:48,088 --> 00:16:51,738
Do not trust a fluent answer
until you check the evidence.

309
00:16:52,118 --> 00:16:55,718
Give support one ZIP for
the whole conversation.

310
00:16:56,508 --> 00:17:01,668
If you remember only three things from the
talk, cost, loops, and grounded answers.

